  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 209 PM EST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis:\n\nExpansive ridging will continue to fold over the eastern half of the \ncountry through the middle of the week, supporting surface high \npressure centered over the Mid-Atlantic that looks to stay locked in \nheading into the weekend. Meanwhile, troughing over the SW CONUS \nwill eject over the Great Plains Wednesday, providing ample forcing \naloft to deepen a cyclone as it treks across the northern Great \nPlains through Wednesday. This system will eventually become \nincreasingly vertically stacked with favorable advections cut off by \nthe second half of the week, leading to weakening as it works across \nsouthern Ontario. Ridging temporarily returns to the Great Lakes by \nthe end of the week before a more active pattern appears it will \nsettle in later this weekend into early next week.\n\n\nForecast Details:\n\nRain-free and seasonably-mild weather is expected across northern \nMichigan through the remainder of this week and to start the \nupcoming weekend. With aforementioned high pressure stretching into \nthe Great Lakes, rain chances will be very low through Friday due to \nlack of substantial moisture and forcing. Breezy southwest winds are \nanticipated over the next several days with gusts to 25-40 mph at \ntimes, however, as the pressure gradient tightens across the region \nwith high pressure centered to our southeast and deepening low \npressure to our west/northwest. This southwest flow will help keep \nmild temperatures in place over the next several days as afternoon \nhighs reach into the 50s and low 60s into Saturday -- which would be \naround 15-20 degrees warmer than average as we head toward the end \nof November. Our next rain chances return late this weekend into \nearly next week as a front may swing across the region, bringing \nback cool temperatures in the 20s and 30s that we're accustomed to \nacross the Northwoods for the week of Thanksgiving.",
